lazychunk_cbytes = sw32_(*chunk + BLOSC2_CHUNK_CBYTES);
```

The `offset` comes from `get_coffset` which reads from the coffsets (compressed offsets). If the offset is out of bounds, `frame->sdata + header_len + offset` could point beyond the buffer, causing a heap-buffer-overflow READ when reading `sw32_(*chunk + BLOSC2_CHUNK_CBYTES)`.

Let me look at `get_coffset` and `get_coffsets` more carefully. In `get_coffset`:
```c
int64_t get_coffset(blosc2_frame* frame, int32_t header_len, int64_t cbytes, int32_t nchunk) {
  int64_t offset;
  uint8_t *coffsets = get_coffsets(frame, header_len, cbytes, NULL);
  ...
  int rc = blosc_getitem(coffsets, nchunk, 1, &offset);
  ...
